data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Research Questions**\n\nI'm currently focused on the structure of the task at hand: answering three research questions, clearly labeled. The key is to address them sequentially, numbering my responses with \"Réponse k,\" corresponding to each question. I'm taking this in stride.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 1118,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 112,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Response Parameters**\n\nI've got a handle on the project's structure. I've defined my response format for these questions and their constraints. I now understand I need to keep the word count per response under 600 words. My aim is now between 450-550 words to avoid any errors. Each response will have the title `Réponse k` where k is the question number. My response must end with a single line: `FIN DES RÉPONSES.`. I will make sure to keep a rigorous and precise tone, making sure to be honest about the citation's certainty and my memory.\n\nI'm now drafting Response 7 (Pièce 7 sur 18). I have the context of observing a sequence of $n$ real numbers $X = (X_1, \\dots, X_n)$ from a process. Two hypotheses $P_1, P_2$ exist, with $m$ reference samples from $P_1$. Marginals of each $X_i$ are identical under $P_1$ and $P_2$, with $X_i \\sim F$ for all $i$.\n\nFIN DES RÉPONSES.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 1491,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 485,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Investigating Dependencies**\n\nOkay, I'm now exploring the implications of differing joint distributions under hypotheses $P_1$ and $P_2$. I'm diving into sub-question 1. Specifically, I'm asking which decision rules have a power ($\\beta$) that differs from the false alarm rate ($\\alpha$). I've got a handle on the finite *n* scenario. For n=1, no test can have differing $\\beta$ and $\\alpha$. For larger *n*, tests based on symmetric or marginal summaries are a no go. Linear combinations of indicators on single variables, or rules dependent on the marginal empirical distribution, won't cut it either.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 1756,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 750,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Clarifying Hypotheses**\n\nAlright, I'm now zeroing in on cases where beta doesn't equal alpha. I've realized tests depending solely on a single observation or a randomized choice of one result in equal $\\beta$ and $\\alpha$. Moreover, tests where the acceptance probability is linear in marginal probabilities also guarantee equality. Specifically, any test based on the form $\\phi(X) = \\sum_{i=1}^n w_i \\psi_i(X_i)$ with specific constraints on the weights and functions will have $\\beta = \\alpha$. Now, by Neyman-Pearson, for n \u003e=2, different copulas for $P_1$ and $P_2$ allow a measurable set A where $P_1(A) \\neq P_2(A)$, which can lead to $\\beta \\neq \\alpha$ as long as $P_1(A)$ is sufficiently different from $P_2(A)$.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2137,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1131,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Exploring Specific Tests**\n\nI'm now considering concrete examples where beta diverges from alpha. I'm focusing on the case where a test rejects the null when $(X_1 - \\mu)(X_2 - \\mu) \u003e c$, and have an example: when X1 and X2 are normally distributed. I'm investigating how finite *m* impacts my analysis, particularly when given as a unstructured set of scalars.\n\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2489,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1483,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Analyzing Detectable Discrepancy**\n\nI'm now diving into sub-question 3, focusing on the minimal detectable discrepancy and separation rate concerning *n* and *m*. I'm assuming shared or known copula families for $P_1$ and $P_2$, such as Markov chains or Gaussian processes. I'm considering scenarios with weakly dependent stationary processes, estimating the marginal *F* from *m* samples and $C_1$ from ordered samples. I'm finding the minimax detectable separation scales as $\\Theta(n^{-1/2} + m^{-1/2})$ for a given test power, and a more complex relationship depending on smoothness for non-parametric copula density.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 2772,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 1766,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Summarizing Literature Findings**\n\nI'm now consolidating my literature review for sub-question 4. I've pinpointed key works. Notably, Sklar's 1959 paper, *Fonctions de répartition à n dimensions et leurs marges*, is foundational. Rémillard & Scaillet's 2009 work in the Journal of Multivariate Analysis, *Testing for equality between two copulas*, provides direct relevance, as does Genest & Rémillard's 2004 contribution on rank-based tests. Ingster's 1993 work is crucial for understanding minimax separation rates.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 3114,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2108,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Defining Simulation Steps**\n\nI'm now outlining a simulation to test various statistical tests. I'll perform 10,000 Monte Carlo runs of an AR(1) Gaussian process with standard normal marginals, transformed via an exponential map, under two parameter settings. I'll compare three tests at different sample sizes: a randomized mixture, a product test with empirical quantile calibration, and a rank-autocorrelation test. I anticipate the simulation will take under ten minutes.\n\n\\n\\n\nI've started drafting response 8, focusing on how a controller can utilize private randomness against a strategic agent. I'm exploring all distinct applications of the private source, particularly in the context of delegated analytical tasks.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 3430,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 2424,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

