data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Organizing Response Elements**\n\nI'm now methodically structuring Response 17, ensuring clarity and completeness regarding the four adversary types: A1, A2, A3, and A4. My focus is the careful organization of each section. This will include quantifying residual damage for each adversary type, given external and internal monitors, including a \"oui/non\" answer with a magnitude estimate. I'll define my defender's design and mechanism, a falsifiable statement, and related literature. I will define the minimum controlled 8B open model experiment, separating true costs from experimental artifacts, and defining the result's scope for each adversary class.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 10023,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9017,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Quantifying Damage Metrics**\n\nI'm now detailing the methodology to measure and refute damage reduction. My focus is on quantifying the synergy gap by defining $D(M, \\alpha)$ as the expected maximum damage given a fixed global false alarm rate, $\\alpha$. I'm considering the combined monitor $M_{1\\lor 2}$, where the synergy gap, $\\Delta D(\\alpha)$, is the difference between the minimum individual monitor damage and the combined approach. The refutable statement is whether $\\Delta D(\\alpha) \u003e 0$ or $\\Delta D(\\alpha) \\le \\epsilon$. I'm assessing the effectiveness of this framework.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 10396,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9390,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Assessing Evasion Mitigation**\n\nI'm now zeroing in on cases of evasion, specifically considering A1's strategy to maintain a hostile setting. Mitigating this with $M_{\\text{int}}$ will activate it strongly, degrading error subtlety. With A2, a light fine-tuning without gradients through $M_{\\text{int}}$ yields a 40-80% relative gain with an out-of-distribution Mahalanobis distance detector. For A3, my answer is no; a low-rank probe design creates no gap. My goal is to use this information.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 10689,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9683,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Refining Synergy Analysis**\n\nI'm now detailing experimental setups to isolate cases, particularly for adversary types A3 and A4. I'm focusing on defining clear attack vectors and how to generate test cases to assess damage reduction, as well as the practical feasibility and the cost to implement these models. I'm aiming to quantify the synergy gap by building test models.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 10987,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 9981,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Prioritizing Literature Review**\n\nI'm now focusing on literature related to stealthy attacks, specifically for the internal/output sensor intersection, drawing from memory to cite Pasqualetti et al. (2013). I'm also recalling the relevance of Carlini & Wagner (2017) and Zou et al. (2023) for machine learning contexts, including how to structure an 8B model to test for these situations. I will now integrate these notes into my model-building.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 11271,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10265,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

data: {"candidates": [{"content": {"parts": [{"text": "**Evaluating Monitor Thresholds**\n\nI'm now detailing a crucial experiment: calibrating the thresholds of monitors $M_{\\text{ext}}$ and $M_{\\text{int}}$ to ensure they have the same false positive rate $\\alpha$ (e.g., 5%) across healthy trajectories. I'm focusing on plotting the Pareto frontier by varying penalty weights and optimization steps. I am also planning to validate the fusion weight $w$ to define its scope.\n\n\n","thought": true}],"role": "model"},"index": 0}],"usageMetadata": {"promptTokenCount": 1006,"totalTokenCount": 11573,"promptTokensDetails": [{"modality": "TEXT","tokenCount": 1006}],"thoughtsTokenCount": 10567,"serviceTier": "standard"},"modelVersion": "gemini-4-argon","responseId": "d_0Ca_6NMK2pxdIP14Hy0Qg"}

